<!--
    RE-friction round 9, need `decompiler-emits-void-scalar` (challenges
    5ab77f5f33c5d40ad448c834 and 6a148dd62b3df128c1df5c9e): a scalar local is
    declared with no width and then used in byte arithmetic.  On `crkme.exe` the
    INT3-detection stub at 0x44ac6b renders

        void v4; // al
        v4 = (void)sub_44ac7a();
        *v1 = v4;
        ...
        v4 = (char)((char)v4 + '$');

    A `void` scalar cannot be declared, assigned or added in C, and the byte the
    stub actually patches into its own callee has no type at all.

    Root cause (P5, type propagation).  `TypeFactory::getTypeCode` builds the
    generic `code` type with size 1 so that `code *` arithmetic steps one byte at
    a time.  `TypeOp::propagateFromPointer` (typeop.cc:207) decides what a
    dereference yields by testing only `ptrto->getSize() == sz`, so it reads that
    convenience as a claim that a byte-wide value can hold a function body: a
    1-byte LOAD or STORE through a recovered function pointer adopts `code` as
    the VALUE type, `INT_EQUAL` spreads it to the compared constant, and the
    merged high is declared `code v4; // al` - printed `void v4;` by the C
    back-end.  The store then demands a cast back to the pointee, which prints
    `(void)`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 "type-propagation".  The P0 assertion
    `option codescalar on|off` decides whether a `code` pointee may type a
    dereferenced value.

    The fixture is the same construct on SysV x86-64.  Both functions take a
    pointer and a flag, zero al, and on the taken arm call something and store al
    through the pointer; they differ only in WHAT they call:

        codebyte:  call *%rbx      # rbx is the call target -> code *
        databyte:  call sink       # rbx is never called    -> plain data pointer

    `codebyte` is the witness.  `databyte` is the discriminator and is identical
    in BOTH passes: nothing types its pointer `code *`, so the byte was always
    `char`.  Pass 1 (`option codescalar off`) reproduces the bug.
-->
